==Requirement Strategies==
*For the first requirement, focus on picking up [[Research Capsules]] and extracting [[Machines]]. The Machine and Capsule count increases the further you descend, so you can earn more {{II|Ichor|name=true}}.
*For the second requirement, have a Distractor [[Toon]] bring {{CI|Twisted Astro|name=true}} to the [[Elevator]] to you so you can get aggro. Playing as {{CI|Rodger|name=true}} also helps getting Research faster because of his Passive [[Ability]]. This will be easier if Twisted Astro is on the [[Daily Twisted Board]].
*For the third requirement, join a run which mainly focuses on Dandy runs. Do '''NOT''' buy from [[Dandy's Shop]] for every even Floor. You can also let a distractor bring {{CI|Twisted Dandy|name=true}} to the Elevator during [[Panic Mode]].

==[[Toon of the Week]] Quests==
{{TOTWQuests|Subdue {{CI|Twisted Astro|name=true}} from his nightmare 5 times.|Recover 10,000 total stamina for any [[toon]] as Astro.|Collect 20 of Toon Astro's Star Pillows.}}

===Rewards===
*1 Quest completed: {{AT|Astro Fan}} [[Title]]
*2 Quests completed: {{SI|Golden Astro|name=true}} [[Sticker]]
*3 Quests completed: {{Skin|Golden Crescent Astro}} [[Skin]]

===Strategies===
*For the first Quest, play until {{CI|Twisted Astro|name=true}} spawns, then allow him to chase you until he falls asleep. Afterwards, run around the map gathering every Twisted Star Pillow to stop the nightmare. Alternatively, if you are playing as Astro, you can use your ability to instantly stop the nightmare without needing to pick up the Twisted Star Pillows. You can do this quest with only one Twisted Astro encounter, so don't worry about having to constantly hunt him.
*For the second Quest, play with Toons with high Stamina, such as {{CI|Brightney|name=true}} or {{CI|Sprout|name=true}}, and have them run around the map constantly, then use your Ability to restore their Stamina. It's highly recommended to equip the {{TI|Star Pillow|name=true}} Trinket to both increase your Star Pillow collectables each Floor as well as your ability's range, and to have your teammates grab as many as they see to further increase the range.
*For the third Quest, enter a run with any player playing as Astro (can either be a teammate or yourself), then run around the floor to search for Star Pillows. It's easier to complete this if there are multiple Astros in the same run, as more collectables spawn.

==Strategies==
Astro excels at Stealth, snowballing and supporting others, so it's important to capitalize on that, considering a below-average [[Health]] stat. Due to having the highest [[Stealth]] in game, Astro can easily support [[Toons]] being chased by using {{AI|Astro|name=true}}, and make a quick getaway without risking damage. On top of that, after or while escaping a [[Twisted]], Astro can regain lost [[Stamina]] much more quickly with his Passive [[Ability]], '''Well Rested''', which gives 3.6/s Stamina regeneration (from 0 Stamina, he would fully regenerate in 34.72~ seconds compared to usual 52.08~ for other Toons with 2 {{Star}} Stamina).

Astro's main weaknesses are his low Health, which is due to him being a {{Type|Main}}, his below average [[Skill Check]] Stat, and his below average Stamina capacity. This means he's above average when it comes to extracting Machines, but can fall behind if you struggle with hitting the somewhat small Skill Checks. Due to this, it is wise to take full advantage of Astro's high Stealth to avoid being chased in the first place, trying not to take any damage, and completing unsafe Machines right underneath Twisted's noses. As long as a Twisted has its eyes set on someone else (besides {{CI|Connie|name=true}} or another Astro, or when the said someone turns a sharp corner around Astro), it will effectively never re-target onto Astro unless he is the only viable target left. This doesn't mean that Astro should always avoid partaking in chases—his higher Stamina regen allows him to last a little longer than other Toons if managed well, so if you find an opportunity to lift a Twisted off of a teammate, don't let fear stop you. Do be careful though, as not only is Astro's capacity below average, but if your teammate is still in the Twisted's circular vision, it may immediately reaggro onto them, resulting in an accidental sabotage in an attempt to save; good positioning and adjusting based on the islands or corners used by your chased teammate should help minimize this risk.

'''Well Rested'''<nowiki/>'s secondary effect should be applied similarly to {{CI|Sprout|name=true}}'s '''Overprotective''': use it to keep tabs on other Toons, considering movement patterns to determine if they need aid or not. Since '''Well Rested''' is not fully reliable, it's less successful than Sprout's strategies, but still has potential. Remember that it kicks in when a Toon reaches 50% Stamina, not zero, so apply '''Nap Time''' depending on the Toon. A Toon like {{CI|Gigi|name=true}} may need immediate attention while a Toon like Sprout could wait until the 30% mark or longer. Always factor in a Toon's [[Movement Speed]] and Stamina Stats; high Stamina/Movement Speed Toons need a refill less desperately than a Toon with low Stamina/Movement Speed. You can also pay attention to the skill levels of your teammates and [[Twisteds]] involved to decide if they're actually at risk. When monitoring others with '''Well Rested''', consider the intricacies of how they are moving (i.e. if they stop sprinting or are moving slowly and the direction) when trying to determine their identity and whether they need help, as well as whether they're being chased. It is helpful to use it when waiting near (but not at) the [[Elevator]] to support teammates low on Stamina, but as '''Nap Time''' can only be applied once during [[Panic Mode]], pay attention to who is still missing, how long they tend to take, and how much percentage of Stamina they have left. If you have already used it but notice stragglers, convey this information to teammates quickly so they can try to assist.

Additionally, '''Nap Time''' permeates barriers. If you cannot run around an obstacle in time, act as if it is not there and get as close to your targets as possible before activating it. This factor is essential when supporting teammates that are distracting {{CI|Twisted Pebble|name=true}} or other significantly fast Twisteds (unless they never break line of sight)—whenever possible, '''do not enter line of sight'''. Stickers can be used to convey your intentions. However, this is where an additional mechanic of Astro's Active comes in, being collectable {{TI|Star Pillow|name=true}}s that spawn around the Floor. If you collect one, it will add a charge to your Active, therefore increasing its range, with a maximum of 10 charges. You can at most use up 5 charges, which creates an incredibly large radius, enough to fill some of the bigger rooms on Floors, and with the potential to easily regenerate the Stamina of a large amount of your teammates. Try to search for the Star Pillows in order to make your Ability more effective, allowing you to avoid having to get too close to distractors. Make sure you don't spend all of your time searching though, as Astro's above average extraction can allow him to contribute with Machines, try to search in between extracting, or on the way to certain locations like the broken Elevator. Other players can also pick these up for you, as they fly directly to their corresponding Astro, so you can request others to pick them up for you if they see them.

'''Nap Time''' can also be used to immediately subdue {{CI|Twisted Astro|name=true}}'s nightmares and put {{CI|Twisted Razzle & Dazzle|name=true}} back to sleep if they've been triggered, making Astro an excellent choice to have around to avoid having to either collect Twisted Astro's Star Pillows or teammates being forced to find alternate routes around Twisted Razzle & Dazzle if they're awake.

As mentioned before, some Toons benefit more from your Ability than others. If you have multiple teammates who are highlighted by '''Well Rested''', or out of Stamina (and they aren’t close enough to each other for your Ability to reach all of them); the generally recommended priority of whom you should help, from highest priority to lowest, is as follows:
*Distractors, or solar distracting {{CI|Bobette|name=true}};
*Toons actively being chased by a Twisted they cannot outwalk, or being chased by a ranged Twisted;
*Toons actively being chased by a Twisted they 'can' outwalk;
*Toons with 2 Star or lower Stamina/3 Star or lower Movement Speed who aren't being chased;
*Toons with 3 Star or higher Stamina/4 Star or higher Movement Speed who aren't being chased;
*Toons extracting from a Treadmill Machine.
Toons far from the Elevator or being chased by Twisteds during Panic Mode can have varying priority depending on their Stats and Abilities, so use your best judgement and knowledge on each Toon's attributes, in order to help in the most effective way. Focusing on slower Toons or those without mobility/survival Abilities is best since they may struggle to get into the Elevator before the timer ends. You should also take into mind your own survival, as if regenerating Stamina of a teammate means your death to Panic Mode, it may not be worth it in the end.

If you struggle in [[Blackouts]], use Skins that have white/light-colored light radii, specifically his {{Skin|Vintage}}, {{Skin|Bedtime Bear}}, or his default Skin. This makes his light radius very effective in Blackouts and makes it easier to see farther. Using any other Skin can make it a bit harder to see in them, with the exception of his Star-Time Skin (since his glow is very similar to his default).

As for [[Items]], Astro doesn't need many Stamina boosts because of his '''Well Rested''' Passive Ability, which is basically a permanent {{II|Stamina Candy|name=false}}. Astro can still take advantage of Stamina boosters (and in fact can do a lot with said Stamina Candies because the buffs are multiplicative), but it's better to leave them for teammates early on. It's best to prioritize Items that help with mobility so that you aren't caught off guard, especially if the below average Stamina capacity causes issues, or Items that will help you with Machine extraction, such as {{II|Jumper Cable|name=false|plural=a}} and {{II|Instructions|name=false}}. Astro also benefits from healing Items, but as he's a Main Character with only 2 max Health, {{II|Bandage|name=false}}s should be prioritized, and {{II|Health Kit|name=false}}s should be left for your non-Main teammates unless you desperately need to heal or they don't need it.

Overall, Astro is a great Toon when it comes to survival, support, and being a stealthy extractor. His struggles with Stamina capacity and Skill Checks can be a bit of an issue, but managing those weakness can allow him to really shine.

